IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `soca-dates`
IMPORT `soca-core`

-- Survival of Causes of Action Act 1940 (SA) -- THE CLAIMANT'S SEAT: the person whose claim meets a death. That is the
-- executor or administrator carrying on the claim of a person who has died (and the person with a dust-related
-- condition who asks what their estate would keep), or the injured person whose claim lies against someone who has
-- died. Every question is asked as that person would ask it, from facts that person can state. The rules are in
-- soca-core.l4; this module restates none of them.
--
-- Based on content from the South Australian Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (version of 8 February 2006).
-- CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `The claimant's questions`

§§ `The claim of a person who has died (ss 2(1)(a), 3)`
-- Situation: someone who had a claim (for example for an injury) has died, and you are their executor or administrator,
-- or will benefit from their estate.

@export The person who died had a claim: does it survive their death for the benefit of their estate (Survival of Causes of Action Act 1940 (SA) s 2(1)(a), (2))?
GIVEN `a cause of action was vested in them at death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died had a right to sue someone (a cause of action) at the time of death, for example for an injury, a debt or a breach of contract
      `it is in defamation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that claim is for defamation (damage to their reputation)
GIVETH A `Survival for the estate answer`
`Does the claim of the person who died survive for their estate` MEANS
    `does it survive for the estate (s 2(1)(a), (2))` `a cause of action was vested in them at death` `it is in defamation`

@export Can the estate of the person who died recover this kind of damages on their surviving claim (Survival of Causes of Action Act 1940 (SA) ss 3(1), (2), (3) and Schedule)?
GIVEN `the head` IS A `Head of damages` @desc The kind of damages asked about: pain or suffering; bodily or mental harm; the curtailment of expectation of life; loss of earning capacity or future earnings for the years the person would have lived but for the wrong; exemplary (punitive) damages; funeral expenses; a loss or gain to the estate that follows from the death (such as insurance paid out or a pension ending); or other damages (for example medical expenses or earnings lost before the death, or damage to property)
      `a cause of action was vested in them at death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died had a right to sue someone at the time of death
      `it is in defamation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that claim is for defamation
      `the wrong caused the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act or omission the claim is about caused the death
      `the condition` IS A `Condition` @desc The person's condition, if the claim is about a dust disease: one of the diseases in the Schedule (aluminosis, asbestosis, asbestos induced carcinoma, asbestos related pleural diseases, bagassosis, berylliosis, byssinosis, coal dust pneumoconiosis, Farmers' lung, hard metal pneumoconiosis, mesothelioma, silicosis, silico-tuberculosis, talcosis), another condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum, or another condition (including an injury that is not a disease)
      `it is attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For another condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum: whether it is attributable to dust
      `they commenced an action for damages for it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person, while alive, commenced an action (started court proceedings) for damages for that condition
      `they died as a result of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died as a result of that condition
      `the action was finally determined before death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that action was finally determined (decided with no appeal left, or ended) before the person died
GIVETH A `Estate damages answer`
`Can the estate recover this kind of damages` MEANS
    `can the estate recover that head (s 3)` `the head` (`does it survive for the estate (s 2(1)(a), (2))` `a cause of action was vested in them at death` `it is in defamation` EQUALS `yes: the cause of action survives for the benefit of the estate of the person who died (s 2(1)(a))`) `the wrong caused the death` (`s 3(2) applies` `the condition` `it is attributable to dust` `they commenced an action for damages for it` `they died as a result of it` `the action was finally determined before death`)

@export I have a dust disease: if I die of it before my court action ends, will my estate still recover damages for pain and suffering, bodily and mental harm, the curtailment of my life expectancy, and exemplary damages (Survival of Causes of Action Act 1940 (SA) s 3(2), (3) and Schedule)?
GIVEN `the condition` IS A `Condition` @desc Your condition: one of the diseases in the Schedule (aluminosis, asbestosis, asbestos induced carcinoma, asbestos related pleural diseases, bagassosis, berylliosis, byssinosis, coal dust pneumoconiosis, Farmers' lung, hard metal pneumoconiosis, mesothelioma, silicosis, silico-tuberculosis, talcosis), another condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum, or another condition
      `it is attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For another condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum: whether it is attributable to dust
      `I have commenced an action for damages for it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have commenced an action (started court proceedings) for damages for the condition
GIVETH A `Dust condition estate answer`
`Will my estate keep those damages if I die of my dust disease` MEANS
    `would s 3(2) keep those damages for the estate` `the condition` `it is attributable to dust` `I have commenced an action for damages for it`

§§ `A claim against a person who has died (ss 2(1)(b), 4, 5)`
-- Situation: the person you have a claim against (for example the driver who injured you) has died.

@export The person I have a claim against has died: does my claim survive against their estate (Survival of Causes of Action Act 1940 (SA) ss 2(1)(b), (2), 5)?
GIVEN `a cause of action existed against them at death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a right to sue them (a cause of action) at the time they died
      `the damage was suffered after or at their death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your damage (for example your injury) happened after they died or at the same time (as in a crash that killed them)
      `a cause of action would have subsisted had they not died first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would have had a right to sue them for that damage if they had not died before or at the same time as it
      `it is in defamation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for defamation
GIVETH A `Survival against the estate answer`
`Does my claim survive against the estate of the person who died` MEANS
    `does it survive against the estate (ss 2(1)(b), (2), 5)` `a cause of action existed against them at death` `the damage was suffered after or at their death` `a cause of action would have subsisted had they not died first` `it is in defamation`

@export The person I have a claim against has died: can I still sue their estate, and am I in time (Survival of Causes of Action Act 1940 (SA) s 4)?
GIVEN `a cause of action existed against them at death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a right to sue them at the time they died
      `the damage was suffered after or at their death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your damage happened after they died or at the same time
      `a cause of action would have subsisted had they not died first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would have had a right to sue them for that damage if they had not died before or at the same time as it
      `it is in defamation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for defamation
      `it is in tort` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is in tort (for example negligence, trespass or nuisance), as opposed to contract or under a statute
      `proceedings were pending at death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had already started court proceedings against them on this claim, still pending when they died
      `the day it arose` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action arose (for a negligence claim, usually the day you suffered the damage)
      `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `a grant has been taken out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their executor or administrator has taken out probate or letters of administration
      `the day of the grant` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration were taken out (any date if none yet)
      `I have taken proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have started court proceedings against the estate on this claim
      `the day I took proceedings` IS A DATE @desc The day you started them (any date if not yet)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Proceedings against the estate answer`
`Can I still sue the estate` MEANS
    `are proceedings against the estate maintainable (s 4)` `a cause of action existed against them at death` `the damage was suffered after or at their death` `a cause of action would have subsisted had they not died first` `it is in defamation` `it is in tort` `proceedings were pending at death` `the day it arose` `the day of death` `a grant has been taken out` `the day of the grant` `I have taken proceedings` `the day I took proceedings` `today`

@export The person I have a tort claim against has died and I had not sued them: by when must I take proceedings against their estate (Survival of Causes of Action Act 1940 (SA) s 4(b): not later than six months after probate or letters of administration are taken out)?
GIVEN `the day of the grant` IS A DATE @desc The day their executor or administrator took out probate or letters of administration
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I take proceedings against the estate` MEANS
    `the last day to take proceedings (s 4(b))` `the day of the grant`

@export The person I have a tort claim against has died and I had not sued them: how far before their death may my claim have arisen and still be brought against the estate (Survival of Causes of Action Act 1940 (SA) s 4(b): not earlier than six months before the death)?
GIVEN `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the earliest day my claim against the estate may have arisen` MEANS
    `the earliest day the cause of action may have arisen (s 4(b))` `the day of death`

§§ `Other rights (s 6)`

@export I am a dependant of the person who died: does their estate's surviving claim take away my own claim for their death (Survival of Causes of Action Act 1940 (SA) s 6(1))?
GIVEN `I am a dependant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a dependant of the person who died (for example their spouse, partner, child or parent who relied on them)
GIVETH A `Dependants answer`
`Does the surviving claim affect my claim for the death` MEANS
    `does the surviving claim affect the dependants' action (s 6(1))` `I am a dependant`

DECLARE `Whose claim` IS ONE OF
    `the claim of the person who died`
    `a claim against the person who died`

@export Can the damages on a claim that survived a death be reduced, or shared, for contributory negligence (Survival of Causes of Action Act 1940 (SA) s 6(2))?
GIVEN `whose claim` IS A `Whose claim` @desc Whether the claim is the claim of the person who died (brought for their estate) or a claim against the person who died (brought against their estate)
      `a cause of action existed at death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the cause of action (the right to sue) existed when the person died
      `the damage was suffered after or at their death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a claim against the person who died: whether the damage happened after they died or at the same time
      `a cause of action would have subsisted had they not died first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a claim against the person who died: whether there would have been a right to sue them for that damage if they had not died before or at the same time as it
      `it is in defamation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is for defamation
GIVETH A `Contributory negligence answer`
`Does the contributory negligence Act apply to the surviving claim` MEANS
    `does the apportionment Act apply (s 6(2))` `it survives`
    WHERE
        `it survives` MEANS
            CONSIDER `whose claim`
            WHEN `the claim of the person who died` THEN
                `does it survive for the estate (s 2(1)(a), (2))` `a cause of action existed at death` `it is in defamation` EQUALS `yes: the cause of action survives for the benefit of the estate of the person who died (s 2(1)(a))`
            WHEN `a claim against the person who died` THEN
                `it survives against the estate` `a cause of action existed at death` `the damage was suffered after or at their death` `a cause of action would have subsisted had they not died first` `it is in defamation`
